<activity xml:id="activity-lift-hand">
	<title>Common description of continuity</title>
	<task><p>Draw a function that is continuous on <m>[0,3]</m>.</p></task>
	<task><p>Draw a function that has a discontinuity on <m>[0,3]</m></p></task>
	<task><p>Draw a function that has a different type of discontinuity on <m>[0,3]</m></p></task>
	<task><p>What do these experiments hint is true about drawing continuous functions.</p></task>
	<task><p>Review the Cantor function (day 1 materials). How does this compare to your guess above?</p></task>
</activity>

<exercise>
	<title>Intervals of discontinuity</title>
	<statement><p>From the examples above it may appear the discontinuities occur solely at a single point or on an interval where the function is undefined. However, there exists functions that are discontinuous on an interval where they are defined.</p>
	<p><md>\text{sp}(x) = \begin{cases} 0 \amp \text{ if } x \text{ is irrational} \\ 1 \amp \text{ if } x \text{ is rational} \end{cases}</md>
	<md>\text{confetti}(x) = \begin{cases} 1 \amp \text{ if } x \text{ is irrational} \\ \frac{1}{p} \amp \text{ if } x=\frac{n}{p^k} n,k \in \Z^+, p \text{ is prime} \\ 0 \amp \text{ otherwise} \end{cases}</md></p>
	<p>Show that the salt and pepper (<m>sp(x)</m>) function is discontinuous everywhere.</p>
	<p>Note, the confetti function is also discontinuous everywhere.</p>
	</statement>
</exercise>

</section>
